// Track I.1c — the closure adapters `map(f)` / `filter(f)`, which call a user closure from inside the
// iterator's `next()`. They are lazy and fuse with every other adapter: a whole `map → filter → take`
// pipeline streams one element at a time with no intermediate list. `map` over an empty/exhausted
// source yields nothing; `filter`'s predicate must return a bool.
// expect: stdout "[10, 20, 30]"
// expect: stdout "[2, 4]"
// expect: stdout "[20, 40]"
// expect: stdout "[16, 25]"
// expect: stdout "15"
// expect: stdout "[]"
// expect: stdout "[(0, 2), (1, 4)]"
// expect: exit 0
xs = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
echo xs.iter().map(fn(n) => n * 10).take(3).collect();
echo xs.iter().filter(fn(n) => n % 2 == 0).collect();
echo xs.iter().filter(fn(n) => n % 2 == 0).map(fn(n) => n * 10).collect();
echo xs.iter().map(fn(n) => n * n).filter(fn(n) => n > 10).collect();
echo xs.iter().map(fn(n) => n + 1).filter(fn(n) => n > 3).sum();
echo xs.iter().drop(10).map(fn(n) => n * 2).collect();
echo xs.iter().filter(fn(n) => n % 2 == 0).enumerate().collect();
